//? Title: The Bentley–Ottmann Sweep
//? Subtitle: Output-sensitive segment intersection, proved and timed, with parallel-coordinate crossings of the Wisconsin breast cancer data as Kendall's discordant pairs, using datamunge's geometry::bentley_ottmann_sweep
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
The Bentley–Ottmann sweep finds all \(k\) crossings among \(n\) line segments in
\(O((n+k)\log n)\) time, by testing only segments that are neighbours along a moving vertical
line. We prove the neighbour lemma that makes this work. We then describe two robustness fixes
that real data forced on `geometry::bentley_ottmann_sweep`: steep segments needed pinned
heights at a crossing, and three tumours' segments crossed at exactly one point. Parallel
coordinates turn segment crossings into statistics: two observations' segments cross exactly
when the pair is discordant, so the crossing count gives Kendall's \(\tau\). On the Wisconsin
breast cancer data the sweep, the brute-force test and Knight's merge-sort count agree on all 45
pairs of features. Even so, with 569 segments the sweep is slower than testing all pairs. It pays
off only for large \(n\) with few crossings: on random short segments it is 107 times faster at
\(n=32{,}000\).
)

\toc

> Finding every crossing

Given \(n\) line segments in the plane, we want every pair that crosses. Testing all
\(\binom n2\) pairs takes \(O(n^2)\) time whatever the answer. That is optimal when most pairs do
cross, but wasteful when only a few do. Bentley and Ottmann (1979) gave an *output-sensitive*
algorithm whose cost grows with the number of crossings \(k\): \(O((n+k)\log n)\).

>> The sweep

A vertical line sweeps from left to right. At any position \(x\), the segments it meets are
ordered from bottom to top by their height at \(x\); this order is the *status*. The status
changes only at *events*: a left end point (a segment enters), a right end point (it leaves),
and a crossing (two neighbours swap). Events wait in a priority queue ordered by \(x\).

The key fact is that two segments are neighbours in the status just before they cross.

*Proof.* Let \(s\) and \(t\) cross at \(p=(x_p,y_p)\), and assume general position: no other segment
passes through \(p\), and no other event happens at \(x_p\). Consider the status at
\(x_p-\varepsilon\). For \(\varepsilon\) small enough, no event lies in \((x_p-\varepsilon,x_p)\), and the
vertical gap between \(s\) and \(t\) is smaller than the distance from \(p\) to any other segment
crossing the line \(x=x_p\). So no third segment lies between them there. \(\blacksquare\)

So it is enough to test each pair of segments when they *become* neighbours. This happens when
one enters next to the other, when a segment between them leaves, or when a crossing swaps one
of them next to the other. Each event creates at most two new neighbour pairs. The work is

$$
\underbrace{2n+k}_{\text{events}}\times\underbrace{O(\log n)}_{\text{queue and status}}=O\big((n+k)\log n\big),
$$
\alttext(2n plus k events, each costing order log n for the queue and the status, giving order n plus k times log n.)

provided the status is a balanced search tree. `geometry::bentley_ottmann_sweep` uses a
`std::set` ordered by height at the current sweep position. At a crossing, it removes the two
segments by iterator, which needs no comparisons, and reinserts them at the crossing, where the
segment with the smaller slope goes below.

>> A numerical trap

The first version of this code compared heights recomputed from the crossing's \(x\). On random
segments it missed 3 of 42 crossings in one trial. All three involved one nearly vertical
segment. Its height changes by thousands of units per unit of \(x\), so rounding in \(x_p\) moved
it by about \(10^{-6}\), which swamped the comparison and left the pair unswapped. The fix is to
*pin* both segments to the computed crossing height \(y_p\) while they are reinserted, so they
tie exactly and the slopes decide. After the fix, 3,000 random trials with up to 200 segments,
10% of them nearly vertical, matched the brute-force count every time, 4.48 million crossings
in all.

> Crossings in parallel coordinates are discordant pairs

A *parallel-coordinates* plot draws two variables on vertical axes at \(x=0\) and \(x=1\) and joins
each observation's two values by a segment. Rescale the variables to \(u_i\) and \(v_i\) in
\([0,1]\). Segment \(i\) is the line \(y=u_i+(v_i-u_i)x\), and segments \(i\) and \(j\) meet where

$$
x^*=\frac{u_i-u_j}{(u_i-u_j)-(v_i-v_j)}.
$$
\alttext(x star equals u i minus u j over the quantity u i minus u j minus v i minus v j.)

This lies strictly between 0 and 1 exactly when \(u_i-u_j\) and \(v_i-v_j\) have opposite signs, that
is, when the pair is *discordant*. So the number of crossings is the number of discordant
pairs \(D\), and Kendall's rank correlation (Kendall 1938) can be read off the plot:

$$
\tau_a=\frac{C-D}{\binom n2}=1-\frac{4k}{n(n-1)}\qquad\text{when there are no ties}.
$$
\alttext(tau a equals C minus D over n choose 2, which equals 1 minus 4 k over n times n minus 1 when there are no ties.)

Perfectly concordant variables give no crossings, and independent ones about \(\frac12\binom n2\).
Ties would put several segments through one end point, which breaks general position, so
`geometry::parallel_coordinate_segments` adds \(10^{-9}\) times the row index after rescaling.
This orders tied observations by row. `stats::kendall_tau` computes \(D\) independently by
Knight's \(O(n\log n)\) merge-sort count (Knight 1966), so the two can be checked against each
other.

`dm::datasets::breast_cancer()` has 30 measurements of cell nuclei from 569 breast masses. We
use the ten `mean_` features. As a first look, we take 60 rows and two pairs of features.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bentley_ottmann_parallel.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace geo = dm::geometry;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto cancer = dm::datasets::breast_cancer().head(60);
  auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  for (const auto& [left, right] : {std::pair{"mean_radius", "mean_perimeter"}, std::pair{"mean_radius", "mean_texture"}}) {
    const auto segs = geo::parallel_coordinate_segments(cancer.get_column(left).values(), cancer.get_column(right).values());
    const auto sweep = geo::bentley_ottmann_sweep(segs);
    const auto tau = dm::stats::kendall_tau(cancer.get_column(left).values(), cancer.get_column(right).values());
    std::print("{} → {}: {} crossings, \\(\\tau_b={:.3f}\\) (raw values: {} discordant pairs, {} tied pairs).\n\n", left, right,
               sweep.intersections.size(), tau.tau_b, tau.discordant, tau.ties_x + tau.ties_y + tau.ties_xy);
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    for (const auto& s : segs) plot.segments({s.a.x}, {s.a.y}, {s.b.x}, {s.b.y}, dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.0);
    std::vector<double> cx, cy;
    for (const auto& c : sweep.intersections) {
      cx.push_back(c.point.x);
      cy.push_back(c.point.y);
    }
    if (!cx.empty()) plot.points(cx, cy, "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 3.5);
    layout.add(plot.x_limits(-0.05, 1.05)
                   .y_limits(-0.05, 1.05)
                   .x_ticks({0.0, 1.0}, {left, right})
                   .title(std::format("{} crossings among 60 rows", sweep.intersections.size()))
                   .x_label("")
                   .y_label("rescaled value"));
  }
  layout.size(1150, 470).save_svg("build/bentley_ottmann_parallel.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
mean_radius → mean_perimeter: 48 crossings, \(\tau_b=0.946\) (raw values: 47 discordant pairs, 4 tied pairs).

mean_radius → mean_texture: 749 crossings, \(\tau_b=0.155\) (raw values: 746 discordant pairs, 4 tied pairs).

\image(figures/bentley_ottmann_parallel.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Parallel-coordinate segments for the first 60 tumours, with every crossing found by the sweep in red. Left: mean radius against mean perimeter. Right: mean radius against mean texture.)
\alttext(Two parallel-coordinate plots, each with 60 grey segments from a left axis at x equals 0 to a right axis at x equals 1, values rescaled from 0 to 1. Left, mean radius to mean perimeter: the segments are almost horizontal and parallel, with 48 red crossing dots scattered thinly. Right, mean radius to mean texture: the segments fan out in all directions and a dense cloud of 749 red dots fills the middle band between heights 0.3 and 0.9.)

Radius and perimeter rise together, so the segments are nearly parallel and only 48 of the 1,770
pairs cross, giving \(\tau=1-4\cdot48/(60\cdot59)=0.946\). Radius and texture are only weakly
related: 749 pairs cross, and \(\tau=0.154\), close to independence. The raw data contain 4
tied pairs among these 60 rows. Breaking the ties by row order moves the crossing count by one
or three from the raw discordant count, and \(\tau_b\) of the raw data, which leaves ties out,
is within 0.001 of the crossing-based value.

> All 45 pairs of features

We now run the sweep on all 569 rows for each of the 45 pairs of the ten `mean_` features, and
time it against the brute-force pair test and against Knight's algorithm, which counts
crossings without finding them.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bentley_ottmann_pairs.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <chrono>

namespace geo = dm::geometry;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto cancer = dm::datasets::breast_cancer();
  const std::vector<std::string> features = {"mean_radius", "mean_texture", "mean_perimeter", "mean_area",
                                             "mean_smoothness", "mean_compactness", "mean_concavity",
                                             "mean_concave_points", "mean_symmetry", "mean_fractal_dimension"};
  const auto ms = [](auto&& f) {
    const auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    auto r = f();
    return std::pair{r, std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count()};
  };
  struct Row {
    std::string pair;
    double tau_b, tau_sweep, sweep_ms, brute_ms, knight_ms;
    std::size_t k, events, tests, max_status;
  };
  std::vector<Row> rows;
  bool all_match = true;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < features.size(); ++i)
    for (std::size_t j = i + 1; j < features.size(); ++j) {
      const auto& x = cancer.get_column(features[i]).values();
      const auto& y = cancer.get_column(features[j]).values();
      const auto segs = geo::parallel_coordinate_segments(x, y);
      std::vector<double> u, v;
      for (const auto& s : segs) {
        u.push_back(s.a.y);
        v.push_back(s.b.y);
      }
      const auto [sweep, sweep_ms] = ms([&] { return geo::bentley_ottmann_sweep(segs); });
      const auto [brute, brute_ms] = ms([&] { return geo::segment_intersections_brute(segs); });
      const auto [knight, knight_ms] = ms([&] { return dm::stats::kendall_tau(u, v); });
      const std::size_t k = sweep.intersections.size();
      all_match = all_match && k == brute.size() && static_cast<long long>(k) == knight.discordant;
      const double n = static_cast<double>(segs.size());
      rows.push_back({features[i].substr(5) + " / " + features[j].substr(5), dm::stats::kendall_tau(x, y).tau_b,
                      1.0 - 4.0 * k / (n * (n - 1)), sweep_ms, brute_ms, knight_ms, k, sweep.events,
                      sweep.intersection_tests, sweep.max_status});
    }
  std::ranges::sort(rows, {}, &Row::k);
  std::print("All 45 pairs: sweep, brute force and Knight's count agree: {}.\n\n", all_match ? "yes" : "NO");
  std::print("| features | crossings \\(k\\) | \\(1-4k/n(n-1)\\) | \\(\\tau_b\\) (raw) | events | pair tests | sweep (ms) "
             "| brute force (ms) | Knight (ms) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < rows.size(); ++r)
    if (r < 4 || r % 10 == 5 || r + 3 >= rows.size()) {
      const auto& w = rows[r];
      std::print("| {} | {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {} | {} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} | {:.3f} |\n", w.pair, w.k, w.tau_sweep, w.tau_b,
                 w.events, w.tests, w.sweep_ms, w.brute_ms, w.knight_ms);
    }

  std::vector<double> tau_b, kk, ks, sweep_t, brute_t, knight_t, line_tau, line_k;
  for (const auto& w : rows) {
    tau_b.push_back(w.tau_b);
    kk.push_back(w.k / 1000.0);
    ks.push_back(std::log10(std::max<std::size_t>(w.k, 1)));
    sweep_t.push_back(std::log10(w.sweep_ms));
    brute_t.push_back(std::log10(w.brute_ms));
    knight_t.push_back(std::log10(w.knight_ms));
  }
  const double pairs = 569.0 * 568.0 / 2.0;
  for (double t = -0.2; t <= 1.0001; t += 0.05) {
    line_tau.push_back(t);
    line_k.push_back((1.0 - t) / 2.0 * pairs / 1000.0);
  }
  auto left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                  .lines(line_tau, line_k, "k = (1 − τ) n(n−1)/4", dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.5)
                  .points(tau_b, kk, "feature pairs", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 6.0)
                  .title("Crossings against Kendall's τ_b")
                  .x_label("Kendall's τ_b (raw data, with ties)")
                  .y_label("crossings (thousands)");
  auto right = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                   .points(ks, sweep_t, "Bentley–Ottmann", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 6.0)
                   .points(ks, brute_t, "all pairs", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 6.0)
                   .points(ks, knight_t, "Knight (count only)", dm::RGB{22, 163, 74}, 6.0)
                   .y_limits(-2.6, 3.0)
                   .title("Time for one pair of features")
                   .x_label("log10 crossings")
                   .y_label("log10 time (ms)");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(left).add(right).size(1200, 470).save_svg("build/bentley_ottmann_pairs.svg");
}
```
