\caption(Left: number of crossings for each of the 45 pairs of `mean_` features against Kendall's \(\tau_b\) of the raw data, with the line \(k=(1-\tau)n(n-1)/4\). Right: time to process one pair on a log scale, by the sweep, by testing all pairs, and by Knight's merge-sort count.)
\alttext(Left: 45 blue points of crossings, in thousands, against Kendall's tau b from minus 0.25 to 0.99, lying on a straight grey line falling from about 101 thousand at minus 0.25 to 0 at 1. Right: log10 time in milliseconds against log10 crossings from 3.1 to 5. The red Bentley–Ottmann points rise steadily from 0 to 2. The black all-pairs points stay between minus 0.35 and 0.2. The green Knight points stay flat near minus 1.25.)

For all 45 pairs, the sweep, the brute-force pair test and Knight's discordant count give
the same number. The crossings run from 1,209 for radius and area, which are almost perfectly
concordant, to 101,162 for area and fractal dimension, the only clearly negative association,
which is 63% of the 161,596 pairs. On the left, every pair lies on the line \(k=(1-\tau)n(n-1)/4\);
the raw \(\tau_b\) and the crossing-based \(\tau\) differ by at most 0.0007.

The timing panel is less flattering. The sweep processes one event per crossing at about 1 µs
each on this machine, so it grows from 1 ms to 97 ms across the pairs. Testing all pairs costs
0.5–1.5 ms whatever \(k\) is, and is faster for every pair here, even the one with only 1,209
crossings. With \(n=569\), \(n^2/2\) cheap tests beat \((n+k)\log n\) operations on a balanced tree
and a heap. Knight's algorithm does better than both, 0.04–0.06 ms, because it only *counts*
crossings using the sorted order and never finds where they are. That is enough for Kendall's
\(\tau\), so the geometric picture is best used for understanding \(\tau\), not for computing it.

> Where the sweep wins

The breast cancer pairs are a hard case for the sweep: with only 569 segments, even 1,209
crossings cost more than testing all 161,596 pairs. The sweep pays off when \(n\) is large and
\(k\) grows more slowly than \(n^2\). Random short segments give a clean family of such cases.
Drop \(n\) segments of length \(\ell\) with uniform centres in the unit square and uniform
directions. Two segments of lengths \(\ell_1,\ell_2\) placed this way cross with probability
\(2\ell_1\ell_2/\pi\) per unit area, ignoring edge effects (Santaló 1976). This is the
two-needle form of Buffon's needle problem. So

$$
\mathbb E[k]\approx\binom n2\frac{2\ell^2}{\pi}\approx\frac{n^2\ell^2}{\pi},\qquad\text{and with }\ell=1/\sqrt n,\quad\mathbb E[k]\approx\frac n\pi.
$$
\alttext(The expected number of crossings is about n choose 2 times 2 ell squared over pi, about n squared ell squared over pi; with ell equal to 1 over root n it is about n over pi.)

With \(\ell=1/\sqrt n\) the crossings grow linearly, so the sweep should take
\(O(n\log n)\) time while the pair test takes \(O(n^2)\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bentley_ottmann_scaling.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <chrono>
#include <numbers>
#include <random>

namespace geo = dm::geometry;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::mt19937_64 rng(2024);
  std::uniform_real_distribution<double> unit(0.0, 1.0);
  const auto ms = [](auto&& f) {
    const auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    auto r = f();
    return std::pair{r, std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count()};
  };
  std::print("| \\(n\\) | crossings \\(k\\) | \\(n/\\pi\\) | sweep (ms) | all pairs (ms) | all pairs / sweep |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> log_n, log_sweep, log_brute;
  (void)geo::bentley_ottmann_sweep({{{0, 0}, {1, 1}}, {{0, 1}, {1, 0}}});  // warm up before timing
  for (std::size_t n = 500; n <= 32000; n *= 2) {
    const double half = 0.5 / std::sqrt(static_cast<double>(n));
    std::vector<geo::Segment> segs;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
      const double cx = unit(rng), cy = unit(rng), angle = std::numbers::pi * unit(rng);
      segs.push_back({{cx - half * std::cos(angle), cy - half * std::sin(angle)},
                      {cx + half * std::cos(angle), cy + half * std::sin(angle)}});
    }
    const auto [sweep, sweep_ms] = ms([&] { return geo::bentley_ottmann_sweep(segs); });
    const auto [brute, brute_ms] = ms([&] { return geo::segment_intersections_brute(segs); });
    if (sweep.intersections.size() != brute.size()) std::print("MISMATCH at n = {}\n", n);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.0f} | {:.2f} | {:.1f} | {:.0f} |\n", n, sweep.intersections.size(),
               n / std::numbers::pi, sweep_ms, brute_ms, brute_ms / sweep_ms);
    log_n.push_back(std::log10(static_cast<double>(n)));
    log_sweep.push_back(std::log10(sweep_ms));
    log_brute.push_back(std::log10(brute_ms));
  }
  const auto slope = [&](const std::vector<double>& y) {
    return (y.back() - y[y.size() - 4]) / (log_n.back() - log_n[log_n.size() - 4]);
  };
  std::print("\nSlopes on the log-log plot over the last three doublings: sweep {:.2f}, all pairs {:.2f}.\n",
             slope(log_sweep), slope(log_brute));
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(log_n, log_brute, "all pairs", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 2.0)
      .points(log_n, log_brute, "", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 6.0)
      .lines(log_n, log_sweep, "Bentley–Ottmann", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2.0)
      .points(log_n, log_sweep, "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 6.0)
      .y_limits(-0.6, 4.3)
      .title("Random segments of length 1/√n: time against n")
      .x_label("log10 n")
      .y_label("log10 time (ms)")
      .size(820, 440)
      .save_svg("build/bentley_ottmann_scaling.svg");
}
```
