\image(figures/bentley_ottmann_scaling.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Time to find all crossings among \(n\) random segments of length \(1/\sqrt n\) in the unit square, by the sweep and by testing all pairs, on log scales.)
\alttext(Log-log plot of time against n from 500 to 32,000. The black all-pairs line rises with slope 2 from about 0.6 milliseconds to 2,670 milliseconds. The red Bentley–Ottmann line starts at the same height at n equals 500 and rises with slope a little above 1 to 25 milliseconds.)

The crossing counts follow \(n/\pi\) closely, for example 10,173 against 10,186 at \(n=32{,}000\);
at \(n=500\) chance and edge effects give 136 against 159. The sweep's time grows with slope
1.17 on the log-log plot, as \(n\log n\) predicts, and testing all pairs with slope 2.03. The two
take about the same time at \(n=500\). By \(n=32{,}000\) the sweep takes 25 ms and the pair test
2.7 s, 107 times longer.

> Summary

- Segments that cross are neighbours in the sweep order just before they cross, so the sweep
  needs only \(2n+k\) events and runs in \(O((n+k)\log n)\) with a balanced-tree status.
- Real data broke two textbook assumptions. Steep segments need their height pinned at a
  crossing to swap reliably, and three breast cancer segments met at one point.
  `geometry::bentley_ottmann_sweep` now handles both and matches brute force on 3,000 random
  trials and all 45 feature pairs.
- In parallel coordinates, crossings are discordant pairs, so \(\tau_a=1-4k/n(n-1)\). The crossing
  counts of the breast cancer features reproduce Kendall's \(\tau\), from 0.99 for radius and
  area to \(-0.25\) for area and fractal dimension.
- Output sensitivity pays only for large \(n\) with \(k\ll n^2\). With 569 segments, testing all
  pairs was always faster. For random segments of length \(1/\sqrt n\), where \(k\approx n/\pi\),
  the sweep was 107 times faster at \(n=32{,}000\). Knight's \(O(n\log n)\) count is the right tool
  for \(\tau\) itself.

> References

- Bentley, J. L. and Ottmann, T. A. (1979). Algorithms for reporting and counting geometric
  intersections. *IEEE Transactions on Computers* C-28(9), 643–647.
